-- ORIGINALLY the discriminating negative control for section 5 (F-3d, #614/#311):
-- a program that carries a typecheck-stage warning which is NOT the demoted
-- coherence one, for a single-file program with no imports.
--
-- WHAT F-3d PINNED (superseded below): `run`/`build` surface `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS`
-- because that diagnostic was LOUD on those verbs before the demotion and would
-- otherwise have gone silent, and F-3d asserted it surfaced NOTHING ELSE --
-- correct at the time because F-3d's allowlist (`runBuildWarnCodes`) was the ONLY
-- gate on `run`/`build` warnings, single-file or not.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS RATHER THAN A CLEAN ONE. The first negative controls
-- for this section were `s3-min-subsumes` and `s8-i2-global-instance-env`, which
-- assert empty run/build stderr -- and they are INERT against a widened filter:
-- neither program produces ANY channel warning, so they read `EMPTY` regardless.
-- THIS file has a real `W-NONEXHAUSTIVE`, so it can actually discriminate.
--
-- THE #1185 SPEW F-3d's NARROWING GUARDED AGAINST is a MODULE-GRAPH phenomenon:
-- `checkGuardExhaustivenessWith` draws its constructor oracle from the whole
-- graph rather than the scrutinee's own type, so an imported constructor
-- universe can make an exhaustive match look partial (measured: `medaka build
-- compiler/driver/medaka_cli.mdk` went from 0 to 4896 stderr lines). This
-- program is SINGLE-FILE with no imports, so it never had an imported
-- constructor universe to draw from -- it was never actually at risk, it just
-- happened to double as the fixture that could tell the allowlist from the
-- whole channel while F-3d's arm covered every verb uniformly.
--
-- UPDATED BY S-warning-parity (#2400/F4): single-file `run`/`build` now filter
-- `isDiagWarn` (the WHOLE channel), not `runBuildWarnCodes` -- deliberately,
-- because single-file has no imported constructor universe and so cannot hit
-- #1185's spew (verified there by experiment: a same-file multi-clause function
-- beside an unrelated multi-constructor type produced no false warning). The
-- narrowing stays in force on the MULTI-MODULE arms, which still can hit #1185.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): nothing in DICT-SEMANTICS is at issue here -- `hue`'s
-- match is genuinely non-exhaustive (no `Blue` arm), which is a §-less exhaustiveness
-- warning, and there is no overlap at all: `impl Sized Colour` is the only impl of
-- its interface. The program is well-typed, `main` calls `hue Red` which IS covered,
-- so it runs and prints 1.
--
-- OBSERVED (current): check ACCEPTS and reports `W-NONEXHAUSTIVE`; run and build
-- ACCEPT, print 1, and now ALSO report `W-NONEXHAUSTIVE` on stderr (F4's
-- intentional single-file widening -- see test/diff_compiler_dict_semantics.sh
-- section 5 for the pinned assertion).
data Colour = Red | Green | Blue

interface Sized a where
  sized : a -> Int

impl Sized Colour where
  sized _ = 7

hue : Colour -> Int
hue c = match c
  Red => 1
  Green => 2

main = println (hue Red)
